<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  One palette, chosen as a family rather than picked per-state, because the
  status strip's whole job is that a glance tells you which state you are in.
  Hues that do not belong together read as decoration; these are one step apart
  in saturation and lightness, so the *difference* between them is the signal.

  Deliberately dark and desaturated: this screen is read outdoors on a phone
  strapped to a controller, where a bright panel is a mirror, and it sits under
  a monospace log that would fight anything louder.

  Colour is never the only channel — every state says the same thing in words
  (MainActivity.renderBanner). Roughly one man in twelve cannot separate the red
  from the green here, and this screen decides whether an aircraft can be moved.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- Commands live: a ground station can move the aircraft. -->
    <color name="state_armed">#B3261E</color>
    <!-- Aircraft's flight controller is talking and the bridge is up. -->
    <color name="state_live">#116149</color>
    <!-- Something is attached but not the thing you want — RC without aircraft. -->
    <color name="state_partial">#8A5300</color>
    <!-- Nothing to report: no RC, or the SDK has not registered yet. -->
    <color name="state_idle">#3A4550</color>
    <!--
      A recorded flight on screen. Deliberately outside the family above: every
      other colour here answers "what is the aircraft doing", and this one answers
      "you are not looking at the aircraft", which is a different question and must
      not be read as a point on the same scale. Violet is the one hue no other
      state uses, and it is never the only channel — the strip says REPLAY in
      words, the picture is watermarked across its whole face, and the withdrawal
      controls grey out.
    -->
    <color name="state_replay">#6A3D9A</color>

    <!--
      A tag the camera can see. Outside the state family above for the same reason
      `state_replay` is: every other colour here answers "what is the aircraft doing",
      and this answers "what can it see", which is a different question. Cyan is the
      remaining hue no state uses.

      Not the only channel: the line says the tag's id and its numbers in words, and
      reads "not seen" when it is not, so the colour is confirmation rather than
      information.
    -->
    <color name="tag_seen">#2AA198</color>

    <!-- The log surface, and the chrome that floats over it. -->
    <color name="terminal_bg">#12161A</color>
    <color name="terminal_fg">#C6D0D8</color>
    <color name="chrome_bg">#1C2329</color>
    <color name="chrome_fg">#E4EAEF</color>
</resources>
